# Line endings, and why one directory needs a different rule.
#
# Most of this repository is ordinary text: git normalises it on commit and hands each platform its
# native endings on checkout. That is what stops a run on Windows from leaving every regenerated CSV
# looking modified.
#
# The exception is the official data.gov.in tables. Each is fetched with its sha256 written into
# data/external/ogd/_manifest.json, and tests/test_data.py hashes the file on disk against it (this is
# finding F-50, which was fixed once in the fetcher and came back at the packaging layer). If git
# rewrites their line endings on checkout, the hash changes and a fresh clone fails its own test suite
# with nothing actually wrong. They are marked -text so they arrive exactly as committed.
#
# The registry export is hashed the same way, but it is a binary .xls and git never converts it.

*                          text=auto
data/external/ogd/*.csv    -text

*.pdf                      binary
*.xls                      binary
*.xlsx                     binary
*.parquet                  binary
*.png                      binary
*.geojson                  binary
